#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074918} :activity/id #uuid "3e2587dd-ee02-59de-894c-e7d3f4d34f27" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049112 :topic/id #uuid "db763a5a-0f9c-54a8-b982-f5ada5dd8c49" :topic/math-academy-id 490} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074717 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186075009 :tutorial/content "When writing a similarity statement, corresponding vertices should appear in the same position in the statement. For example, if we're given that\n\n$$\nABC \\sim PQR\n$$\n\nfor two polygons $ABC$ and $PQR$, we can immediately deduce that\n\n$$\n\\angle A \\cong \\angle P, \\qquad \\angle B \\cong \\angle Q, \\qquad \\angle C \\cong \\angle R\n$$\n\nWe can also deduce that\n\n$$\nk = \\dfrac{AB}{PQ}= \\dfrac{BC}{QR} = \\dfrac{AC}{PR}\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is the similarity ratio." :tutorial/id #uuid "28869083-9c33-5d75-a178-1a1624e17f5e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4721 :tutorial/title "Correctly Writing Similarity Statements"} :step/id #uuid "0907e75c-35e4-5576-acda-f1ed2bced1b9" :step/math-academy-id 29154 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074921}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074918 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074983 :tutorial/content "Two polygons are **similar** if they have the same shape (though they may have different sizes).\n\nMore precisely, two polygons are similar if the following conditions hold:\n\n1. Their corresponding angles are congruent.\n2. The ratios of the lengths of their corresponding sides are all equal.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's consider the triangles $\\triangle ABC$ and $\\triangle EFG$ below.\n\n![](images/23/233fcb591a81d9ee6eef3caf1f4df0f0e959a18144177e89d5f0ac88c94525f4.png)\n\nFrom the figure above, we see that the corresponding angles are congruent:\n\n$$\n\\angle A \\cong \\angle E, \\qquad \\angle B \\cong \\angle F, \\qquad \\angle C \\cong \\angle G\n$$\n\nWe also need to check whether the ratios of the corresponding sides are the same. That is, we have to check whether\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{AB}{EF} &= \\frac{BC}{FG} = \\frac{AC}{EG}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the lengths of the sides, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{AB}{EF} &= \\frac{6}{12} = \\frac{1}{2} \\\\\n\\frac{BC}{FG} &= \\frac{4}{8} = \\frac{1}{2} \\\\\n\\frac{AC}{EG} &= \\frac{5}{10} = \\frac{1}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe ratios are the same. Therefore, we conclude that the triangles $\\triangle ABC$ and $\\triangle EFG$ are similar. The common ratio of the sides,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nis called the **similarity ratio**.\n\nTo express mathematically that the triangles $\\triangle ABC$ and $\\triangle EFG$ are similar, we write\n\n$$\n\\triangle ABC \\sim \\triangle EFG\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "d16f60d4-ecf1-552e-82e5-d75e3cee2d89" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2312 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "64268339-5582-581d-80ef-13e6ca1ae5d8" :step/math-academy-id 9636 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074919}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074919 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073912 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073598 :question/id #uuid "da7af116-26c8-56e4-a6e5-b195cbb7699e" :question/math-academy-id "e-2968"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6447abce-804f-5569-90ef-32d989cc7a06" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050481} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050563}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074215} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074216}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Similarity Ratio Between Two Similar Polygons"} :step/id #uuid "1357d4d0-01b0-55ba-8726-66e975b8fddf" :step/math-academy-id 9637 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074920}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074920 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073913 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073599 :question/id #uuid "fd7f22e7-9aba-5936-beb1-56d3a5e016cc" :question/math-academy-id "e-2969"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "63b3448f-fe75-5152-a3c0-00b5129375c5" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050164} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050585} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051086}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074230} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074231}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Shapes Similar to a Given Polygon"} :step/id #uuid "481134bc-9f38-5984-aff1-9db66ee8acb8" :step/math-academy-id 9638 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074717}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074921 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073914 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073600 :question/id #uuid "98596871-dc8e-5083-a773-1a70ffe1a159" :question/math-academy-id "e-2970"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "77da466d-77c4-5ea7-bedb-753601111c40" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049223}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074202} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074217}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Correct Similarity Statement Between Two Similar Polygons"} :step/id #uuid "47e04ca3-a7fb-543e-b432-6fd5034d97c7" :step/math-academy-id 9640 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074922}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074922 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073915 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073601 :question/id #uuid "8fda1604-bea3-5061-9a3f-a0ec86f95a41" :question/math-academy-id "e-2972"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dca2a1be-4373-5572-9cf4-3c634079b1e9" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074218} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074221}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Possible Lengths of Sides for a Polygon Given Dimensions of a Similar Polygon"} :step/id #uuid "ede808b5-83e2-593c-9171-40086c5b7fa3" :step/math-academy-id 9641}] :activity/title "Similarity and Similar Polygons" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186073925}]}
